Heritage 
The Andaman and Nicobar Islands are rich in heritage and historical sites, particularly from the era of British colonial rule and the Indian independence movement.

Cellular Jail, 
Cellular Jail, located in Sri Vijaya Puram, South Andaman district, is a historic colonial prison and one of India’s most important freedom struggle monuments. Built by the British in 1906, it was used to exile political prisoners during the struggle for independence. Today, it stands as a solemn reminder of India’s fight for freedom and is a major tourist and educational site. 
https://tourism.andamannicobar.gov.in/userpages/admin/uploads/heritage/1756084563_12151200.JPG 
The Cellular Jail, also known as Kala Pani, is one of the most important historical landmarks of the Andaman & Nicobar Islands. Built by the British in 1906, this massive colonial prison was used to exile Indian freedom fighters, subjecting them to solitary confinement and inhumane conditions. Today, the Cellular Jail has been converted into a National Memorial and Museum, showcasing India’s painful past and the bravery of countless revolutionaries. 
xhibits:

Freedom Fighters’ Gallery – Photographs, personal belongings, and records of revolutionaries.
Prison Cells – Small, isolated cells that show the harsh solitary confinement system.
Torture Instruments – Artifacts and replicas depicting the brutal treatment of inmates.
Paintings & Sculptures – Artistic representations of the struggle for independence.
Audio-Visual Room – Documentaries and films on the freedom movement.
Light & Sound Show – A moving experience narrating the sacrifice of martyrs (held in evenings).
