Port Blair: A tapestry of rich culture and tradition
An art haven 
https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/andaman-and-nicobar-islands/sri-vijaya-puram/a-tapestry-of-rich-culture-and-tradition 
Port Blair, the capital of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, is a vibrant city teeming with life and artistic expression. Its rich history and diverse cultural influences have fostered an exceptional arts scene, where tradition and modernity blend seamlessly at every corner. Delve into the heart of this enchanting city to experience the array of artistic expressions waiting to be discovered.

Dance
The art of dance flourishes in Port Blair, where various traditional and contemporary forms coexist in harmony. One such tradition is the Nicobari dance, which hails from the indigenous communities of the Nicobar Islands. This ceremonial dance, accompanied by melodic songs, showcases rich local customs and folklore. To witness these mesmerising performances at their best, visit cultural events such as Island Tourism Festival or Subhash Mela.


Music
The musical tapestry of Port Blair is as vibrant as its diverse population, blending local sounds with global influences. The islanders have a deep-rooted love for music and one can often hear folk tunes resonating throughout the city streets. Tourists can experience local music at cultural centres like Jawahar Navodaya Vidyalaya and ANIIDCO Auditorium, as well as at live performances during festivals celebrating tribal art and culture.

Painting
Art enthusiasts visiting Port Blair will find a thriving community of painters whose works showcase both traditional themes and contemporary styles. Reflecting on the islands' natural beauty and rich history, these paintings capture the essence of life in Andaman and Nicobar. Visit art galleries such as Lalita Kala Parishad Art Gallery or conduct research about local artists exhibiting their work in various venues around the city to truly appreciate this picturesque art form.

Literature
The literary scene in Port Blair is strewn with stories of adventure, survival, love, and triumph that are deeply rooted in their island setting. Local authors such as Sunil M. Agrawal, Ram Kapse, and B.B. Choudhury have penned marvellous tales that explore the lives and experiences of the islanders. The Port Blair library adjacent to Marina Park offers a comprehensive collection of literature from these regional writers, along with the works of other notable Indian and international authors.

Theatre
Port Blair boasts a lively theatre culture that reflects its rich historical past and diverse community influences. Residents often come together to stage engaging plays and performances inspired by their unique lives within the islands. To immerse yourself in Port Blair's theatre scene, attend shows at cultural institutions like Rabindra Manch Theatre, Andaman Club, or check local listings for upcoming performances by local theatre troupes.

Textiles
The intricate textile craftsmanship found in Port Blair is an ode to the island's rich culture and artistic heritage. Visitors can find one-of-a-kind garments, accessories, and home décor items featuring traditional embroidery and weaving techniques passed down through generations of artisans. For an authentic shopping experience, explore Sagarika Emporium, Aberdeen Bazaar or visit local markets where independent artisans display their wares.

*Rubber Plantations in Wandoor
https://www.incredibleindia.gov.in/en/andaman-and-nicobar-islands/sri-vijaya-puram/rubber-plantations-in-wandoor 
The largest, the most scenic plantations
Rubber is one of the most significant cultivations on the island and there are many spots where you can observe the manufacturing process. But, it is best showcased in the vast rubber plantations of Wandoor. These are the most scenic plantations, nestled amidst the lush greenery of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, offering a captivating glimpse into the world of rubber cultivation. Covering an expansive area of approximately 931 hectares, these plantations are the largest in Andaman.

The plantations, with around ten thousand rubber trees, are a testament to sustainable agriculture in the Andaman archipelago. Wandoor's location in the South Andaman region provides the ideal climate for rubber cultivation, ensuring robust growth and high-quality latex production.

