Zampa Gateway
https://dnhddtourism.in/diu/place-to-visit/Other%20Attractions/zampa-gateway 
Zampa Gate in Diu, from the Portuguese era, showcases intricate architecture and served as a significant town entrance. Named after an olive tree, it reflects Diu's historical charm and is a tourist attraction.

Zampa Gate is one of the prominent landmarks in Diu, a small island town off the coast of Gujarat, India. This historical structure dates back to the Portuguese colonial era and is a well-preserved example of their architectural influence in the region. The gate is characterized by its impressive architecture, featuring intricate carvings and sturdy construction.

Zampa Gate holds significant historical importance as it served as one of the primary entrances to the town during the Portuguese rule. The name "Zampa" is derived from the Portuguese word "Zambujeiro," which means olive tree. The gate was named after a huge olive tree that once stood nearby.

Today, Zampa Gate stands as a reminder of Diu's rich heritage and attracts tourists interested in history and architecture. It's often included in guided tours of the island, offering visitors a glimpse into its colonial past.
